WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.963 --> 00:00:02.081
- That's yours.

00:00:02.081 --> 00:00:03.000
- This is mine.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:03.833
- Look at that.

00:00:03.833 --> 00:00:06.648
(audience cheering)

00:00:06.648 --> 00:00:09.740
And I like how it hugs the back.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.063
- You like how it hugs the back?

00:00:11.063 --> 00:00:11.896
- I like how it hugs the back

00:00:11.896 --> 00:00:14.310
'cause like, I gotta show the waist off.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:15.143
So, I like that.

00:00:15.143 --> 00:00:16.174
Yeah.

00:00:16.174 --> 00:00:17.470
- Yes, and on a good
day you can hug the back

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.360
and on a bad day you can take the belt off

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.144
and just let it not hug anything.

00:00:21.144 --> 00:00:23.110
- And just let it feel like a Snuggy.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:23.943
I love it.

00:00:23.943 --> 00:00:24.950
It's nice to see you again.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.424
- Nice to see you. Hi.

00:00:26.424 --> 00:00:27.300
- Yeah, so I heard that you recently

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.230
made it back to "Wicked"

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.190
but you were watching it this time.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:32.270
- Yes, I have never gone back to see.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:33.360
But one time I went back

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.390
and my son was three and
he was so rambunctious,

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:38.280
he kept hitting the backseat
of the people in front of me.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.883
So I had to leave and I wrote a letter.

00:00:39.883 --> 00:00:40.780
- And you were like, maybe too early.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:41.930
- Yes. And I wrote a letter

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.300
to the women that were in it apologizing,

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.053
because you know if they heard
that somebody had viewed...

00:00:47.053 --> 00:00:48.600
I mean, if I left in
the middle of the show,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.433
they would think that I...

00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:50.450
- Oh, of course. Of course.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.470
- Yeah.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:52.610
- That's happened to me before.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.720
Something happened in my family
and I had to leave a show,

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.090
and then, I guess it
got back to them I left,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.140
and I was like, oh my God, it
had nothing to do with you.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:00:59.973
- [Idina] Like you didn't like them.

00:00:59.973 --> 00:01:00.806
- Exactly.

00:01:00.806 --> 00:01:01.639
- Yeah

00:01:01.639 --> 00:01:04.510
But I was there with
seven 12 year old boys,

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.990
who are all my son and
his basketball team.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.350
His coach is actually from the east coast

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.183
and has been telling them

00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:12.200
that they they're too west
coast, they're too soft.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.070
They have to meet all
of the New York kids.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.161
- [Kelly] Oh my God.

00:01:15.161 --> 00:01:15.994
- So they were scrimmaging.

00:01:15.994 --> 00:01:18.000
I brought them all on a
plane and then I was like,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.680
where can I take them Saturday
night to a Broadway show?

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.930
Oh, "Wicked" will give me tickets.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.310
And I took them and they were riveted.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:25.520
They just sat there the whole time.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.883
They were just so into it.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.650
Some had seen "The Wizard
of Oz," some hadn't.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:30.610
But it was just...

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:31.470
- [Kelly] Wait! What?

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:32.303
- [Idina] Yeah.

00:01:32.303 --> 00:01:33.790
- How do you miss that as a child?

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.580
- I tried to show my son,

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.560
but he just wanted to watch
dinosaurs, or whatever.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.393
- Oh my God.

00:01:38.393 --> 00:01:39.400
I didn't even give mine the option.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.930
I was like, wait, this part's real good.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.210
- [Idina] But, it's scary.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.043
- [Kelly] It is a little scary

00:01:43.043 --> 00:01:44.180
- A little scary.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.470
- Except my kids like
the scary characters.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.515
Like my kids gravitate towards
the Ursula's or the Gaston's.

00:01:49.515 --> 00:01:50.348
- Well now he does.

00:01:50.348 --> 00:01:52.030
He loves scary now, for sure.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:52.863
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:54.458
- Like he loves his
T-Rex's, that kind of thing.

00:01:54.458 --> 00:01:55.390
- [Kelly] Yeah. Oh my God,
my kids love dinosaurs.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.372
Both of them.

00:01:56.372 --> 00:01:57.205
- Yeah.

00:01:57.205 --> 00:01:59.390
- Does your son think that
you're cool generally or not?

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:00.360
It's kind of hard.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.240
- Yeah, you know, he
doesn't think I'm cool.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.570
I'm not like this.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.510
Like I'm not allowed to say the word drip,

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.277
drippy or whatever,
because that's his word.

00:02:08.277 --> 00:02:09.160
- What do you mean?

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.090
- He says things like,
"this is so drippy mom."

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:13.320
He's really into his fashion
and all his basketball.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.110
- Is like trippy and dope, in the same?

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:15.943
- Kind of.

00:02:15.943 --> 00:02:17.500
So what I'll say, he'll
come out ready for school

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.410
and be like, "whoa, that is so drippy."

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.257
He'll be like, "no, you don't get to say."

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:24.702
- That's my vocabulary.

00:02:24.702 --> 00:02:26.033
- Now I've just said it
on national television.

00:02:26.033 --> 00:02:27.180
He's gonna die.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.523
Mom why would you say drippy?

00:02:29.523 --> 00:02:30.356
(Kelly laughing)

00:02:30.356 --> 00:02:31.260
I said drippy.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:32.513
And then the other day,

00:02:33.521 --> 00:02:36.050
I was going to pick him up from school

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:37.770
and I was too lazy to go up the stairs

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:38.610
to get my own sneakers,

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.790
and I'm finally in the
same size sneakers as him.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.580
Or he's finally in the
same sneaker size as me.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.323
And I guess he had,

00:02:47.409 --> 00:02:49.837
I spoiled him and I got him some Yeezy's

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:52.940
for Christmas last year.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.479
- Oh, Okay, yeah.

00:02:54.479 --> 00:02:55.350
- And I was like, oh,
I've never tried these.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.130
So I put them on and I went
and picked him up from school,

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.317
and he comes out and he goes,
"How could you wear those?"

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.820
He said, "without socks,

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.027
do you know how expensive those are?"

00:03:07.027 --> 00:03:09.420
I was like, "yeah, that's why
I thought I could put them on

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.261
and go and pick you up from school."

00:03:11.261 --> 00:03:12.094
- Because I bought them.

00:03:12.094 --> 00:03:12.927
- [Idina] Yes.

00:03:12.927 --> 00:03:13.980
- I love that he really just didn't want

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.360
you sweating in them.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.193
Just ruining them.

00:03:16.193 --> 00:03:17.650
- My feet are so gross!

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:18.483
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:03:18.483 --> 00:03:19.316
- I changed your diaper,

00:03:19.316 --> 00:03:21.299
but I can't put my feet in your shoes.

00:03:21.299 --> 00:03:22.460
- Yeah, I've wiped your
butt multiple times.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.160
Build a bridge, get over it.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:24.993
So wait,

00:03:24.993 --> 00:03:26.730
you also worked with your
sister on a children's book.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:27.563
- Yeah.

00:03:27.563 --> 00:03:28.660
- That's so exciting.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.040
What made you wanna do that?

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.263
- Well, I don't know.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:33.840
I wanted to work with my sister.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.770
I wasn't sure about doing
another book, you know?

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:40.250
Like, how lots of people put
out picture books and stuff.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:41.960
But the idea of doing it with my sister

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.870
who's an elementary school
teacher and a writer,

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:45.100
and we're super close.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.421
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:46.421 --> 00:03:47.670
- And it just was the
most organic experience.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:48.730
- [Kelly] And you're such a
hit with kids that age too,

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:49.563
learning to read.

00:03:49.563 --> 00:03:50.460
- Yeah. Yes.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:51.697
- And your kids read a lot, I heard.

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:52.623
- Oh, Like a lot.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.278
It's forced family fun, Idina.

00:03:56.278 --> 00:03:58.458
- (laughing) It's forced family fun.

00:03:58.458 --> 00:03:59.380
- Forced family fun.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:00.440
I mean, now they're used to it,

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.540
but in the beginning it
was not exciting for them.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:03.666
- I know.

00:04:03.666 --> 00:04:05.330
- Well, I brought you a
very, like an early copy.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:06.250
It's like the soft cover

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.780
before they've even got
the hard covers made.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.700
But it's called "Loud Mouse"

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.750
and it's about a little
mouse with a big loud mouth,

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:15.928
like me.

00:04:15.928 --> 00:04:17.180
(Idina and Kelly laughing)

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.427
- Your sister was like, I
have the perfect character.

00:04:19.427 --> 00:04:20.260
- Yeah.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.220
She's like this really
annoying older sister mouse,

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:23.660
it never shuts up.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:24.493
And then there's a little...

00:04:24.493 --> 00:04:25.450
It's kind of about that.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:29.590
But yeah, it's just about
celebrating your unique gifts

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.650
and not being afraid to
share them with the world.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:33.630
- Thank you for a new book.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.590
We've gone through, literally
we read every night,

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:37.960
so I have such a huge children's library

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:38.820
and then we pass them on.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:39.880
- [Idina] Oh, good. Good.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.110
- Thank you for the new one.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:43.120
If I read, like, "The Good Dinosaur"

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:44.180
like all these other things again...

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:45.050
- I love "The Good Dinosaur."

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:47.710
- Me too. Me too.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:49.434
It's just a lot of times.

00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:50.610
(Idina laughing)

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:51.790
I can, like, recite it.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:52.900
- I know.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.700
That's why I like the Richard Scarry books

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.410
because you can like...

00:04:57.262 --> 00:04:58.095
Do you know those books?

00:04:58.095 --> 00:04:58.928
- I don't know.

00:04:58.928 --> 00:05:00.160
- oh, maybe I'm dating myself.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:01.257
They're like before your time.

00:05:01.257 --> 00:05:03.155
- No, they're not,
we're like the same age.

00:05:03.155 --> 00:05:03.988
- I'm gonna get those for you now.

00:05:03.988 --> 00:05:04.821
No, we're not the same age.

00:05:04.821 --> 00:05:06.470
But I'm gonna get those books for you.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:09.373
- [Kelly] What?

00:05:09.373 --> 00:05:11.320
- I know, I'm 51 in like a couple days.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:12.153
- What?

00:05:13.038 --> 00:05:16.038
(audience cheering)

00:05:18.746 --> 00:05:20.060
I just turned 40.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.590
I thought you were literally my exact age.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:25.603
What fountain are you drinking from?

00:05:25.603 --> 00:05:29.500
- It's not. It's a lot of
makeup and people helping me.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.979
- Congratulations.

00:05:30.979 --> 00:05:32.184
- [Idina] Oh, thank you so much.

00:05:32.184 --> 00:05:33.017
- I mean, on the book and all the things,

00:05:33.017 --> 00:05:34.542
but like more on that.

00:05:34.542 --> 00:05:35.384
- [Idina] Aww.

00:05:35.384 --> 00:05:36.217
- Like that's great.

00:05:36.217 --> 00:05:37.410
Congratulations.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:38.710
Wait, did you say your
birthday's coming up?

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:39.800
- Yeah, May 30th.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:40.900
- [Kelly] Oh, that's exciting.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:41.860
- Yeah, yeah.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.060
- Oh, you get trumped by
Memorial Day every year.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:44.893
That's that weekend, right?

00:05:44.893 --> 00:05:46.211
- Yeah, I used to always.

00:05:46.211 --> 00:05:48.300
And because it's a holiday weekend

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.420
I always was gigging and
working on that weekend.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:51.253
- And even when you were young

00:05:51.253 --> 00:05:52.560
people were probably
out with their families

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:53.393
for birthday parties.

00:05:53.393 --> 00:05:54.510
- Nobody wanted, it's
a big summer weekend.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:55.930
- I kind of took it
negative, your birthday.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:57.458
I don't know why I did that. I don't know.

00:05:57.458 --> 00:05:58.390
- That's okay. (laughing)

00:05:58.390 --> 00:05:59.905
It is negative, it's 51!

00:05:59.905 --> 00:06:01.654
- Your birthday is so awesome.

00:06:01.654 --> 00:06:04.040
No, I literally this whole time

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:05.230
thought we were exactly the same.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:06.729
- Well, I'm flattered.

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:10.020
- So you once said that the
scariest part of performing

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:11.200
is like the three seconds

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:12.330
like right before the curtains come up.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:13.170
- Did I say that?

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:14.210
Probably.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:15.900
- [Kelly] So, you do? It's amazing.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.500
- Do you still have that kind of stuff?

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:18.533
- Here's the thing.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:22.000
I've never been like stagefright,

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.150
or I've never had like
that kind of nerves,

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.300
but like I grew up seeing classical stuff

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.000
so you have to be perfect
in all those auditions.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:28.833
- That's how I feel,

00:06:28.833 --> 00:06:31.145
and with a theater community as well.

00:06:31.145 --> 00:06:32.310
- You have to be perfect.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:33.930
But it started taking the joy away for me.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.180
And this one night, it was actually funny.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:37.748
It was in Australia,
like this random show,

00:06:37.748 --> 00:06:38.820
and I was miserable

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.880
because I had focused
on these minor things

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.980
that I just didn't nail, like vocally.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.300
And then I noticed the joy of like singing

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.730
was like leaving me.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.135
- [Idina] Leaving, uh-huh.

00:06:49.135 --> 00:06:50.390
- Yeah. And so then, it's interesting.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:52.750
I was like, you know what, like, whatever

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:54.880
like, I'm gonna sound like
soulful and pitchy sometimes.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:55.970
It's gonna be fine, and whatever.

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:57.760
And I feel like my voice got better.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:59.240
I feel like I became better

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.463
because you got out of your head.

00:07:00.463 --> 00:07:01.591
- [Idina] You've got to relax.

00:07:01.591 --> 00:07:02.650
- Yeah. But do you get that?

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:03.810
- Oh my God, I get so in my head

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:05.700
and I feel like the older I'm getting,

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:10.210
I'm getting a little bit more neurotic.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.230
I don't know if that's with
success and social media,

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:15.143
and feeling like the lens is on me.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.532
- But they're on you for a reason.

00:07:19.532 --> 00:07:20.399
- Yeah, I know.

00:07:20.399 --> 00:07:21.978
- They're not on you because you suck.

00:07:21.978 --> 00:07:23.420
- I'm not gonna lie,

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.082
Like I just, I do.

00:07:25.082 --> 00:07:27.020
- A lot of my friends in the
industry, they get nervous.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:30.200
- But I will say that
becoming a mom helped me

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.400
because I felt the need to be perfect

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.353
was not as crazy.

00:07:35.677 --> 00:07:36.510
- It's not the most important thing now.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:37.343
- It's not.

00:07:37.343 --> 00:07:38.690
I mean, if my son has a fever,

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:41.070
I'm not gonna not be with him
in the middle of the night

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:41.903
before I have to get up.

00:07:41.903 --> 00:07:42.900
So then I'd get on stage

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:44.270
and I'd be like you screw it up tonight,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:45.920
well whatever, you had to make a choice.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:48.526
So if you don't hit that big
note, you know, big deal.

00:07:48.526 --> 00:07:49.678
- That's me too. Yeah, that's me too.

00:07:49.678 --> 00:07:50.762
But then you just alter the melody.

00:07:50.762 --> 00:07:51.595
- [Idina] And then I was better.

00:07:51.595 --> 00:07:52.428
- Yeah.

00:07:52.428 --> 00:07:53.261
- [Idina] Yeah, you have
to alter the melody.

00:07:53.261 --> 00:07:54.094
- You have to alter the
melody. You have to handle it.

00:07:54.094 --> 00:07:55.880
My band will tell you right now,

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.499
I am the queen of walking in and going,

00:07:58.499 --> 00:08:01.157
"oh, it's so interesting.
Are we gonna lower this?"

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:04.810
Are we gonna? oh yeah, just keep going.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:06.170
Or like, sometimes I have to take it up.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:07.210
It's just wherever your voice is.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:08.510
- Exactly.

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:10.270
- I alter melodies, I change.

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:11.103
- [Idina] Me too.

00:08:11.103 --> 00:08:12.493
- You have to if you're a live singer.

00:08:12.493 --> 00:08:14.497
- I have a thing called
the ABC Performance.

00:08:15.565 --> 00:08:17.280
So especially when I'm doing
like eight shows a week,

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.190
if you're really tired and stuff.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:20.540
I've decided there's A show,

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:23.220
which is the stuff that the
composer really wants you to hit

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.320
when the critics are there.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:25.322
- Yeah.

00:08:25.322 --> 00:08:26.530
- And then you come up with
a couple alternate melodies

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:28.737
that no one would even
know that you changed.

00:08:28.737 --> 00:08:30.290
But in your head psychologically,

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:31.550
you feel like you're failing yourself

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:33.570
because you're getting sick
and you think, oh I can't.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.790
But if you just give yourself
this particular thing

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:38.330
and then you nail that B show or C show

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:40.280
in a beautiful way,
with all of your heart,

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:41.810
people feel just as much

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:44.100
and you don't feel completely devastated

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.830
that you didn't hit some high
notes for people, you know.

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.890
- Because you're human and it's a muscle,

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:50.392
by the by, so yeah.

00:08:50.392 --> 00:08:51.810
But you're fabulous,

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:52.770
so you should always remember that.

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:53.890
So those three seconds,

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:55.970
just think of me saying you're awesome.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:57.070
- I will.

00:08:57.070 --> 00:08:58.240
That's what I was gonna say.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.000
I was thinking 30 seconds before,

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.720
well I just imagine I'm Kelly Clarkson.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:03.553
- No imagine you're you

00:09:03.553 --> 00:09:04.386
and I'm just sitting there,

00:09:04.386 --> 00:09:06.340
like so no matter if you suck
you're still gonna be awesome.

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:07.502
Just so you know.

00:09:07.502 --> 00:09:08.855
- I'm going to think that, thank you.

00:09:08.855 --> 00:09:10.340
- Yeah, you should think
that. I'm just saying.

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:13.590
So, we've all had shows that
didn't go the way we planned.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.650
So have you ever had a show
that you were like, oh no,

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:18.320
like something bad happened?

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:19.350
- So the Menzel girls,

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.688
